"The i-th alien currently has A_i stones, and will become an adult exactly i years later."
"When someone becomes an adult on this planet, every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."

Let's trace the sample 1 to understand the timing and who gives stones.
$N=4$.
$A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Initially (Year 0):
Alien 1: 5 stones.
Alien 2: 0 stones.
Alien 3: 9 stones.
Alien 4: 3 stones.
Adults: None.

Year 1:
Alien 1 becomes an adult.
Who are the adults? The problem says "every adult who has at least one stone".
Before year 1, no one is an adult. So no one gives stones to Alien 1.
Alien 1 has 5 stones.
State after Year 1:
Alien 1: 5 stones.
Alien 2: 0 stones.
Alien 3: 9 stones.
Alien 4: 3 stones.
Adults: Alien 1.

Year 2:
Alien 2 becomes an adult.
Adults present: Alien 1.
Alien 1 has 5 stones ($\ge 1$). So Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 2.
Alien 1 now has $5-1 = 4$ stones.
Alien 2 receives 1 stone.
Alien 2 now has $0+1 = 1$ stone.
State after Year 2:
Alien 1: 4 stones.
Alien 2: 1 stone.
Alien 3: 9 stones.
Alien 4: 3 stones.
Adults: Alien 1, Alien 2.

Year 3:
Alien 3 becomes an adult.
Adults present: Alien 1, Alien 2.
Alien 1 has 4 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1 stone.
Alien 2 has 1 stone ($\ge 1$). Gives 1 stone.
Alien 3 receives $1+1=2$ stones.
Alien 1 now has $4-1 = 3$ stones.
Alien 2 now has $1-1 = 0$ stones.
Alien 3 now has $9+2 = 11$ stones.
State after Year 3:
Alien 1: 3 stones.
Alien 2: 0 stones.
Alien 3: 11 stones.
Alien 4: 3 stones.
Adults: Alien 1, Alien 2, Alien 3.

Year 4:
Alien 4 becomes an adult.
Adults present: Alien 1, Alien 2, Alien 3.
Alien 1 has 3 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1 stone.
Alien 2 has 0 stones ($< 1$). Gives 0 stones.
Alien 3 has 11 stones ($\ge 1$). Gives 1 stone.
Alien 4 receives $1+0+1=2$ stones.
Alien 1 now has $3-1 = 2$ stones.
Alien 2 now has $0$ stones.
Alien 3 now has $11-1 = 10$ stones.
Alien 4 now has $3+2 = 5$ stones.

